    <!-- ================================================================
         IA REDESIGN — CYCLE 5. THE RAIL CUTOVER.
         ================================================================

         The rail is FIVE sections and EIGHT items:

           (overview)   Home
           OPERATE      Pay Run · People · Workforce
           UNDERSTAND   Insights · Compliance
           GROW         Learn
           (system)     Settings

         Everything else on this rail was absorbed by one of those eight and is
         RETIRED here: `active` false takes an item off the rail, and the record
         moves into the 900+ band because a deactivated item still OCCUPIES its
         sequence for W8's uniqueness rule (W18) — the moment an admin re-enables
         one, a duplicate would matter, and after this cutover almost every
         retired item's old number belongs to something else.

         NOTHING IS DELETED, and every retired item still points at a live
         action. W76 qualified "retire, never delete": the rule is only worth
         anything while re-enabling the item would still WORK. It does — this
         cycle removed no cockpit and no client action, so every one of these
         records is one `active` flag away from coming back, and
         `pb_sidebar/tests/test_ia_c5.py` asserts each one still resolves.

         WHERE THE EIGHT ITEMS LIVE. Not here. Each hub ships its own
         `pb.sidebar.item` in its own module (`pb_home_hub/data/pb_sidebar.xml`
         and friends), which is the convention `pb_mission`, `pb_audit`,
         `pb_tenants`, `pb_bank_ocr` and `pb_young_worker` already follow. The
         alternative — declaring all eight here — would put a rail entry on a
         database whose hub module is not installed, pointing at an action that
         does not resolve: an admin's click answered with nothing (W79). The
         SECTIONS stay here, because a section belongs to the rail rather than
         to any one surface.
         ================================================================ -->

    <!-- ============ Sections ============
         Five live sections; the other five are retired with their items. The
         `technical_key` moves with the name where the meaning moved, because a
         section keyed `payrun` and labelled OPERATE is a rail that reads one way
         and reports another — and `pb_demo.post_init_demo` is the one consumer
         that reads these keys (its `_LOCK_SECTION_KEYS`), so the remap is
         accounted for there in the same cycle.

         Section sequences are unique too: two sections on one number order
         themselves by id, i.e. by whichever module happened to install first,
         which is W70's defect one level up. GROW is 40 and lives in `pb_learn`
         (its data file is GENERATED — regenerate with
         `docs/tutorial_poc/author/tools/gen_learn_data.py`, never hand-edit),
         which is why nothing here claims 40. -->
    <record id="sec_overview" model="pb.sidebar.section">
        <field name="name">Overview</field><field name="technical_key">overview</field>
        <field name="sequence">10</field><field name="show_label" eval="False"/>
    </record>
    <!-- WAS "Pay Run" / `payrun`. The section that used to hold ten pay-run
         items now holds the three missions you spend a working day inside. -->
    <record id="sec_payrun" model="pb.sidebar.section">
        <field name="name">Operate</field><field name="technical_key">operate</field>
        <field name="sequence">20</field><field name="show_label" eval="True"/>
    </record>
    <!-- WAS "Insights" / `insights`. Insights and Compliance are the two
         missions you open to find something out rather than to change it. -->
    <record id="sec_insights" model="pb.sidebar.section">
        <field name="name">Understand</field><field name="technical_key">understand</field>
        <field name="sequence">30</field><field name="show_label" eval="True"/>
    </record>
    <!-- WAS "Admin" / `admin`, six items. One now: the Settings cog's hub, which
         is the one door to every configuration surface in the product (Cycle 3).
         `show_label` is false, as OVERVIEW's is: a one-item block does not need
         a heading, and the rail's top and bottom are the two places a heading
